			<div class="content" id="post-content-4785522">
									

<blockquote>
<p>• If there are weird or unusal spaces, explicitly state what aspects of the space defy physical laws or don't.<br />
&#8230; If a substance is unidentified, it should be analyzed and the results should be given because that's the point of a report. I</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I suspect you're conflating what an SCP article is (a piece of flash-fiction) for what it simulates (a scientific report). Those familiar with reports of this nature might notice these oversights, but &#8212; much as the target audience for <em>Iron Man</em> isn't an actual engineer &#8212; the target audience for SCP articles aren't actual scientists (though I'm sure some scientists enjoy reading them!). The goal is to create a simulacra convincing enough to fool a layman&#8230; not reproduce a 'genuine' report.</p>
<p>There's wiggle room here, obviously; different articles extend more (or less) deeply into 'authenticity'. But I strongly disagree that the elements you're pointing out here represent fundamental errors. Missed opportunities, <em>maybe</em> &#8212; but I don't think most people reading this would take issue that a substance is simply described as 'unidentified'.</p>

							</div>

								
					
